\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pathfinder-note}

\title{Participation Accounting and Learning Incentives in Comparison-Based Reward Shaping}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{The pair and the connexion pursued}

Q develops mechanisms for agents with privately known preferences and
capabilities, requiring incentives for truthful reporting and obedient
action. P introduces MARS-RA, which aggregates an external multimodal
model's pairwise comparisons into contribution scores and uses their
potential differences to guide cooperative multi-agent learning with
changing participation.

The connexion investigated was whether Q could ground strategic
robustness for P's comparison-based rewards. The investigation instead
separated complete-return incentives from finite-time learning,
identified a conditional participation-accounting problem, and
constructed harmful learning updates under accurate comparisons and
restricted critics. \ledger{1,2,8,20}

This account retains the previous note's accounting results, backbone
audit, critic-error decomposition, exact-credit constructions,
effective-game experiments, projection characterization, selective
guidance, neural tests, and query-noise analysis. It appends the fifth
round's visual experiments, task-return audit, and additional
prior-work limits. The latest verifier review is entry 46. It accepted
the earlier calculations but returned ITERATE because their mathematics
had close precedents, the selective example separated components by
construction, and the neural experiments had limited critic training
and unmatched input classes across some controls. It requested a
visual intervention with matched inputs and a realistic critic budget,
plus an audit that did not require harmful-component labels.
\ledger{34,46}

The new visual experiments do not reproduce the earlier delay collapse.
They show possible transient guidance, substantial seed variation,
and residuals often dominated by unpredictable query noise. The
task-return audit identifies a local directional derivative when
feedback suffices, but cannot certify useful guidance from absent
task evidence. Additional prior work further narrows novelty.
Evidence about P remains thin: no failure is established in MARS-RA,
and the record does not support a publication-ready contribution.
\ledger{48,51,53,56,57,58}

\section{Results retained from the first round}

\subsection{Statistical consistency does not establish incentives}

P's acting agents do not submit contribution reports to its comparator:
an external LMM evaluates egocentric images. Recovering that
comparator's latent scores does not establish incentives for truthful
disclosure or desirable actions. Q's peer-discipline construction
instead requires separation of beliefs over co-player types,
cancellation of reported intrinsic utility, and unrestricted rewards
or prohibitions. Comparison accuracy supplies none of these
conditions. The proposed direct justification of P through Q is
therefore rejected. \ledger{2,8}

Q's obedience condition compares prescribed behavior with every
feasible unilateral deviation; see \url{inputs/Q.tex},
lines 1568--1604. Its peer construction appears at lines 1648--1732.
P's comparisons, score estimation, and shaping appear in
\url{inputs/P.tex}, lines 285--349. Its Shapley interpretation assumes
that the comparator's latent scores are Shapley values; statistical
consistency does not establish that premise. See
\url{ada/participation_shaping.md}, lines 7--13, and
\url{ada/round3_effective_contests.md}, lines 17--23.
\ledger{2,24,31,36}

\subsection{Complete accounting preserves payoff comparisons}

Fix a persistent player \(i\), horizon \(T\), discount
\(\gamma\in(0,1]\), and shaping weight \(\rho\). Let
\(\psi_{i,t}\) be the realized potential, with \(\psi_{i,T}=0\),
and define
\[
F_{i,t}=\gamma\psi_{i,t+1}-\psi_{i,t}.
\]
Adjacent transitions must use the same cached realization of each
potential.

\begin{claim}
If every transition's shaping reward enters the persistent player's
discounted return, then
\[
\rho\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^tF_{i,t}
=\rho\bigl(-\psi_{i,0}+\gamma^T\psi_{i,T}\bigr)
=-\rho\psi_{i,0}.
\]
A strategy-independent initial potential leaves every complete-return
deviation gain unchanged.
\end{claim}

The proof is cancellation of adjacent discounted potentials. It
requires no comparison-accuracy assumption and remains valid when
later scores or membership depend on strategic choices. With
information and feasible strategies held fixed, it preserves best
responses and equilibria. This shaping layer alone cannot create
Q-style alignment incentives, although it can change finite-time
learning. This is established potential-based reward shaping, not a
new theorem. The assumptions and argument appear in
\url{ada/participation_shaping.md}, lines 15--43.
\ledger{3,7}

\subsection{Activity masking can create an entry charge}

Suppose instead that the return recorder retains only
\(m_{i,t}F_{i,t}\), where \(m_{i,t}\in\{0,1\}\) indicates current
participation. Reindexing gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^tm_{i,t}F_{i,t}
={}&-m_{i,0}\psi_{i,0}\\
&+\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}
\gamma^t(m_{i,t-1}-m_{i,t})\psi_{i,t}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Under the explicit convention that inactive agents have zero
potential, exit terms vanish. Define
\[
E_i=\{t:1\leq t<T,\ m_{i,t-1}=0,\ m_{i,t}=1\}.
\]
Then
\[
\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^tm_{i,t}F_{i,t}
=-m_{i,0}\psi_{i,0}
-\sum_{t\in E_i}\gamma^t\psi_{i,t}.
\]

For active agents, use P's softmax construction:
\[
\psi_{i,t}
=\frac{\exp(c_{i,t})}
{\sum_{j:m_{j,t}=1}\exp(c_{j,t})}.
\]
An empty coalition has zero potential; a singleton has potential one.
These are completion conventions, not implementation details verified
in P. An entering agent loses the incoming positive shaping payment
but retains the subsequent debit. An additional entry at time \(e\),
with potential \(q\), reverses an otherwise comparable base-return
advantage \(\Delta\) whenever
\[
0<\Delta<\rho\gamma^eq.
\]
This is a conditional accounting result; see
\url{ada/participation_shaping.md}, lines 45--67.
\ledger{4,5}

\subsection{An exact counterexample and correction}

The constructed game has two agents, one always present. The focal
agent chooses between staying active and resting for one step before
returning. Rest supplies an additional base reward \(1/10\) at time
two. All active comparisons are ties. Set \(\gamma=99/100\) and
\(\rho=1\):
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
& (m_{i,0},m_{i,1},m_{i,2})
&(\psi_{i,0},\psi_{i,1},\psi_{i,2},\psi_{i,3})\\ \hline
\text{stay}&(1,1,1)&(1/2,1/2,1/2,0)\\
\text{rest}&(1,0,1)&(1/2,0,1/2,0).
\end{array}
\]
Complete accounting gives both paths shaping return \(-1/2\), so rest
is preferred by \(0.09801\). Masking adds an entry charge of
\(0.49005\), giving
\[
\begin{aligned}
U_i^{\mathrm{masked}}(\text{stay})&=-0.5,\\
U_i^{\mathrm{masked}}(\text{rest})&=-0.89204.
\end{aligned}
\]
The agent rejects beneficial rest despite exact comparisons. This
replaces P's battery mechanics with a controlled rest choice; it is
not a reproduction of P's simulator. \ledger{5,6,10}

Under zero inactive potentials, define
\[
\begin{aligned}
\widehat F_{i,0}&=m_{i,0}F_{i,0},\\
\widehat F_{i,t}
&=m_{i,t}F_{i,t}
 +(m_{i,t}-m_{i,t-1})\psi_{i,t},
 \qquad 1\leq t<T.
\end{aligned}
\]
The correction refunds the entry charge on the first outgoing active
transition. Substitution yields
\[
\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^t\widehat F_{i,t}
=-m_{i,0}\psi_{i,0}.
\]
It restores the payoff comparison, assuming persistent preferences
across absences and a common discount clock. \ledger{5,6}

The construction and correction appear in
\url{ada/participation_shaping.md}, lines 69--96.
The checks in \url{ada/boundary-check-001.log}, lines 1--10,
verify 254 zero-inactive presence paths and 32 additional paths for
the general identity. Subsequent source review identifies the
correction as an application of known episodic boundary effects,
not independent mathematical novelty. \ledger{5,14}

\subsection{Composition with Q's incentive margins}

If a separate mechanism gives the target strategy base margin
\(\delta_i(d)\) over deviation \(d\), and \(B_i\) is the discounted
accounting residual, the new margin is
\[
\delta_i(d)
+\mathbb E[B_i\mid\text{target}]
-\mathbb E[B_i\mid d].
\]
A strategy-independent residual preserves every margin. If its expected
value varies across strategies by at most \(\varepsilon_i\), strict
margins greater than \(\varepsilon_i\) survive, whereas weak
implementation can fail under an arbitrarily small adverse change.
This is a composition rule, not an implementation theorem for unknown
preferences. See \url{ada/participation_shaping.md},
lines 100--108. \ledger{8}

\subsection{Information and initial-report qualifications}

Revealing private evaluator information through observed rewards
changes the information structure. Payoff telescoping alone then
does not establish equilibrium equivalence. Likewise, a report chosen
before the initial potential is set leaves the report-dependent
payment
\[
-\rho\psi_{i,0}(\hat t_i,\hat t_{-i}).
\]
A score increasing in reported contribution can reward understatement.
Including the reporting transition from a fixed initial potential,
or compensating that boundary, removes this effect. P has no such
reporting stage and uses comparisons during training; these
qualifications concern extensions into online mechanisms. See
\url{ada/participation_shaping.md}, lines 41 and 110--112.
\ledger{7}

\section{Results retained from the second round}

\subsection{The public backbone audit weakens the masking hypothesis}

The audit examined public XuanCe and standard MAPPO code, not
MARS-RA's unavailable shaping implementation or a confirmed revision
used by P. At XuanCe revision
\url{63eca3216bd87055de55ff89e94fe64f31a80e3d},
rewards, terminal flags, and activity masks are stored separately.
Return and GAE recursions use rewards and terminal flags; activity
masks are applied later to actor and critic losses. Temporary exit
could cut returns if the environment adapter marks it terminal,
but an activity mask alone does not do so. \ledger{12}

The relevant files under
\url{ada/audit_sources/agi-brain__xuance/} are
\url{xuance/torch/agents/core/on_policy_marl.py},
lines 143--149;
\url{xuance/common/memory_tools_marl.py},
lines 210--234; and
\url{xuance/torch/learners/multi_agent_rl/mappo_learner.py},
lines 59--110.
The audit and immutable links appear in
\url{ada/round2_findings.md}, lines 21--38.

At standard MAPPO revision
\url{de66d7a4b23fac2513f56f96f73b3f5cb96695ac},
the shared SMAC runner separates individual activity masks from return
masks, setting the latter to zero at whole-environment termination.
SMAC is not a respawn environment, but this distinction again blocks
the inference from inactive actor updates to deleted rewards.
See \url{ada/round2_findings.md}, lines 40--48. \ledger{12}

For the rest example, complete shaped rewards and persistent
discounted returns are
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\widetilde r_0,\widetilde r_1,\widetilde r_2)
&=(-0.5,\ 0.495,\ -0.4),\\
(R_0,R_1,R_2)
&=(-0.40199,\ 0.099,\ -0.4).
\end{aligned}
\]
Dropping the inactive actor update at time one does not remove its
reward from \(R_0\). Marking exit terminal instead changes \(R_0\)
to \(-0.5\), but also removes the future rest benefit from the
unshaped objective. This is a different termination convention,
not evidence of a shaping-specific entry charge.
The scalar recursion is checked in
\url{ada/learning-channels-001.log}, lines 34--35.
No implementation-bug claim is established. \ledger{12,19}

\subsection{Team sums remove comparison content}

Embed active shares into the persistent population, assigning zero to
inactive agents. Let \(z_t\) indicate a nonempty, nonterminal coalition.
Then
\[
\sum_i\psi_{i,t}=z_t,
\qquad
\sum_iF_{i,t}=\gamma z_{t+1}-z_t.
\]
Comparison content cancels from complete team-sum shaping even when
membership changes. With nonempty coalitions before termination, the
sum is \(\gamma-1\) on nonterminal transitions and \(-1\) on the final
transition. Its discounted total is \(-z_0\). \ledger{13}

Current-active summation instead gives
\[
\sum_{i\in I_t}F_{i,t}
=\gamma z_{t+1}-z_t
-\gamma\sum_{i\in I_{t+1}\setminus I_t}\psi_{i,t+1}.
\]
A scalar critic trained on complete summed rewards receives no ranking
content through that reward. Separate agent outputs can receive it
through individual shaped rewards. Per-agent policy gradients need
not cancel, because each agent multiplies its own advantage by its
own policy score. Team-sum cancellation therefore does not establish
that P's shaping is ineffective. See
\url{ada/round2_findings.md}, lines 78--109.
\ledger{13,19}

\subsection{Separating accounting changes from critic mismatch}

Consider \(H\) rollout transitions beginning at \(t\), with no
intermediate termination, consistent cached potentials and discounts,
and zero terminal potentials and values at an actual terminal state.
Write
\[
\widetilde r_t-r_t
=\rho(\gamma\psi_{t+1}-\psi_t)+b_t,
\qquad
e_t=\widetilde V_t-V_t+\rho\psi_t,
\]
where \(b_t\) is an accounting modification and the values are actual
critic predictions. Subtracting temporal-difference residuals gives
\[
\widetilde\delta_t-\delta_t
=b_t+\gamma e_{t+1}-e_t.
\]
Summing with generalized advantage estimation weights yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
\widetilde A_t-A_t
={}&\sum_{\ell=0}^{H-1}(\gamma\lambda)^\ell b_{t+\ell}-e_t\\
&+\gamma(1-\lambda)
  \sum_{\ell=1}^{H-1}(\gamma\lambda)^{\ell-1}e_{t+\ell}\\
&+\gamma(\gamma\lambda)^{H-1}e_{t+H}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Complete accounting and matched predictions
\(\widetilde V=V-\rho\psi\) leave sample advantages identical for every
\(\lambda\). For a complete terminal rollout with \(\lambda=1\), only
the current prediction error remains. If that term is independent of
the current action conditional on actor history, it contributes zero
to the expected policy gradient; sample variance and clipped updates
need not coincide. These are consequences of established GAE and
shaping identities. See \url{ada/round2_findings.md},
lines 111--144. \ledger{14,15,16,19}

The rest calculation distinguishes return correction from
learning-update correction. At \(\lambda=0.95\), its GAE advantage is
\[
\begin{array}{l|rr}
\text{Accounting}
&\text{Base critic}
&\text{Exact recorded-return critic}\\ \hline
\text{Complete}&0.073260&0.098010\\
\text{Activity masked}&-0.3922875&-0.392040\\
\text{Entry corrected}&0.049982625&0.098010
\end{array}
\]
Here the base critic is used without a shaping adjustment. Refunding
the boundary restores returns but re-times payments, so it need not
restore GAE under unmatched critics. The masked exact-critic advantage
crosses zero at undiscounted rest benefit \(0.5\), precisely the
threshold \(\Delta=\rho\gamma^2/2\). The sweep appears in
\url{ada/learning-channels-001.log}, lines 2--33; line 1 records
1,000 successful randomized identity checks. \ledger{19}

\subsection{Exact Shapley credit can induce harmful learning updates}

Two cooperative agents choose \(x_i\in\{0,1\}\) at time zero.
A common task reward is paid on the final transition, with no further
decisions. Define
\[
\begin{aligned}
v_x(\varnothing)&=0,\\
v_x(\{i\})&=1+a x_i,\\
G(x)=v_x(\{1,2\})&=2-d(x_1+x_2).
\end{aligned}
\]
Take \(a=0.5\) and \(d=0.002\). The game is monotone in coalition
inclusion, although not necessarily superadditive. Action \(1\)
improves stand-alone production but reduces common reward by \(d\),
representing resource congestion. \ledger{15}

Its exact Shapley values satisfy
\[
\begin{aligned}
c_i(x)
&=1+\frac{a-d}{2}x_i-\frac{a+d}{2}x_j,\\
c_i(x)-c_j(x)&=a(x_i-x_j).
\end{aligned}
\]
Use an oracle comparator with Bradley--Terry probabilities generated
by these values, equivalently the population estimation limit:
\[
q_i(x)=\sigma\bigl(a(x_i-x_j)\bigr),
\qquad
\sigma(u)=\frac{1}{1+\exp(-u)}.
\]
For two transitions, initial potential \(1/2\), and terminal zero,
\[
\widetilde r_{i,0}=\rho(\gamma q_i-1/2),
\qquad
\widetilde r_{i,1}=G-\rho q_i.
\]
Every complete shaped return equals \(\gamma G-\rho/2\).
Action \(1\) remains strictly worse in the return game. See
\url{ada/round2_findings.md}, lines 146--182. \ledger{15}

Restrict the terminal-step critic to aggregate production \(G\),
omitting individual rank information. Under independent actors with
the same action probability \(p\),
\[
\mathbb E[q_i\mid G]=\frac12.
\]
The population-optimal squared-error predictors are
\[
V(G)=G,
\qquad
\widetilde V_i(G)=G-\frac{\rho}{2}.
\]
The base critic is exact, while the shaped critic cannot represent the
individual offset despite being fully fitted within its feature class.
Its mismatch is \(e_{i,1}=\rho(q_i-1/2)\). Additional optimization
with the same features cannot remove it. \ledger{17,18}

With an exact initial baseline, the expected GAE difference between
action \(1\) and action \(0\) is
\[
\Delta A_i
=\gamma\left[
-d+\rho(1-\lambda)\bigl(\sigma(a)-1/2\bigr)
\right].
\]
For a Bernoulli logit policy,
\[
\mathbb E[(x_i-p)\widehat A_i]
=p(1-p)\Delta A_i.
\]
Thus the harmful action is reinforced exactly when
\[
0<d<\rho(1-\lambda)\bigl(\sigma(a)-1/2\bigr).
\]
At \(\rho=1\) and \(\lambda=0.95\), the upper bound is approximately
\(0.006123\), exceeding \(d=0.002\). Both agents' gradients have the
same sign, so parameter sharing does not cancel the effect.
\ledger{15,17,18}

Individual credit as a critic feature permits the predictor
\(G-\rho q_i\), restoring base advantages. Setting \(\lambda=1\)
also removes this expected bias in the complete-rollout example.
A critic recovering fraction \(\alpha\) of the individual offset
replaces the threshold by
\[
d<\rho(1-\alpha)(1-\lambda)
       \bigl(\sigma(a)-1/2\bigr).
\]
This concerns expected updates under specified critic predictions,
not a changed equilibrium of correctly shaped returns or an
established feature of P. See
\url{ada/round2_findings.md}, lines 184--228.
\ledger{15,17,20,22}

The population-regression experiment refits the critic at every
enumerated expected-gradient update. Starting at \(p=1/2\), with
\(\gamma=0.99\) and logit step size \(2\), 4,000 updates give
\[
\begin{array}{l|rr}
\text{Condition}&p&\mathbb E[G]\\ \hline
\text{Unshaped}&0.082538&1.999670\\
\text{Shaped, aggregate critic}&0.963196&1.996147\\
\text{Shaped, rank-conditioned critic}&0.082538&1.999670
\end{array}
\]
The implementation is \url{ada/test_projected_critic.py},
lines 19--68; results are in
\url{ada/projected-critic-001.log}, lines 10--13.
The small welfare difference deliberately isolates a small incentive
margin. These numbers verify the construction, not performance on P's
benchmark. \ledger{18}

\subsection{Direct comparison payments and obedience}

Paying \(Bq_i\) directly changes preferences. For two agents,
\[
\frac{\partial(Bq_i)}{\partial c_i}=Bq_i(1-q_i),
\qquad
\frac{\partial(Bq_i)}{\partial c_j}=-Bq_i(1-q_i).
\]
Increasing one's measured score and decreasing a rival's score have
symmetric marginal rewards. Such payments alone cannot distinguish
productive effort from sabotage or manipulation of visible evidence.
A comparison-only contract also cannot distinguish actions preserving
the entire distribution of comparison observations.

If the target has intrinsic advantage \(\delta_i(d)\), obedience
requires
\[
B\left(
\mathbb E[q_i\mid d]-\mathbb E[q_i\mid\text{target}]
\right)\leq\delta_i(d)
\]
for every deviation. In the congestion example,
\[
B\bigl(\sigma(a)-1/2\bigr)\leq d.
\]
The two-step approximate update has the same threshold with effective
prize \(B=\rho(1-\alpha)(1-\lambda)\). This connects the calculation
to Q's deviation inequalities without importing its unrestricted
utility cancellation, state contracts, reports, or prohibitions.
The direct-payment calculation is classical contest logic. See
\url{ada/round2_findings.md}, lines 244--267.
\ledger{19,20}

\section{Results retained from the third round}

\subsection{A delayed reward generates an effective contest}

There are \(n\geq2\) agents choosing actions simultaneously at time
zero, followed by no further decisions. A common reward \(G(x)\) is
paid on transition \(H-1\), with \(H\geq2\). Set the initial potential
to \(1/n\), each intermediate potential to
\[
q_i(x)=\frac{\exp(c_i(x))}{\sum_j\exp(c_j(x))},
\]
and terminal potential to zero. Complete accounting gives
\[
\widetilde R_i(x)=\gamma^{H-1}G(x)-\rho/n.
\]
Delay changes no complete-return ranking of action profiles.
\ledger{25,31}

Assume exact base intermediate predictions and shaped predictions
\[
\begin{aligned}
V_{i,s}(x)&=\gamma^{H-1-s}G(x),\\
\widetilde V_{i,s}(x)
&=V_{i,s}(x)
-\rho\left[\alpha q_i(x)+\frac{1-\alpha}{n}\right],
\qquad 1\leq s<H.
\end{aligned}
\]
Initial predictions differ by \(-\rho/n\), terminal values are zero,
and \(\alpha\in[0,1]\) measures the represented individual offset.
Fully fitted aggregate critics have \(\alpha=0\) when
\[
\mathbb E[q_i\mid\text{critic features}]=1/n.
\]
Identical independent policies and symmetric aggregate features provide
this property. Rank-conditioned or explicitly shifted critics can
attain \(\alpha=1\). \ledger{25,31}

At intermediate times,
\[
e_{i,s}=\rho(1-\alpha)(q_i-1/n).
\]
The GAE identity gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\widetilde A_{i,0}-A_{i,0}
={}&\rho(1-\alpha)\gamma(1-\lambda)\\
&\quad{}\times
\sum_{s=1}^{H-1}(\gamma\lambda)^{s-1}(q_i-1/n).
\end{aligned}
\]
Up to the positive scale \(\gamma^{H-1}\) and action-independent
baselines, the expected initial score-function update is the policy
gradient of
\[
U_i^{\mathrm{eff}}(x)=G(x)+\beta_Hq_i(x),
\]
where
\[
\beta_H=
\frac{\rho(1-\alpha)\gamma(1-\lambda)}
     {\gamma^{H-1}}
\sum_{s=1}^{H-1}(\gamma\lambda)^{s-1}.
\]
The finite sum avoids a singular geometric-series expression at
boundary parameters. The prize vanishes for a matched critic or
\(\lambda=1\). For \(\gamma=1\),
\[
\beta_H=\rho(1-\alpha)(1-\lambda^{H-1}).
\]
For \(\gamma<1\), delay can increase the prize relative to the
discounted terminal objective. \ledger{25,26,31}

This is a gradient representation with critic predictions held fixed,
not an equilibrium-equivalence theorem for the original return game.
The fitted aggregate prediction has this form along exchangeable
policies; arbitrary asymmetric policies need not preserve it.
Subsequent PPO steps with active clipping are not exactly gradients
of this utility. See \url{ada/round3_effective_contests.md},
lines 25--92. \ledger{31,32}

\subsection{Obedience and information lost by normalization}

Let \(x^\star\) maximize \(G\). For unilateral deviation \(y_i\), write
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Delta G_i
&=G(x^\star)-G(y_i,x_{-i}^\star),\\
\Delta q_i
&=q_i(y_i,x_{-i}^\star)-q_i(x^\star).
\end{aligned}
\]
The target is an equilibrium of the effective game exactly when
\[
\Delta G_i\geq\beta_H\Delta q_i
\quad\text{for every }i,y_i.
\]
It survives every nonnegative prize exactly when no unilateral
deviation increases the acting agent's share. For finite actions,
the admissible prize bound is the infimum of
\(\Delta G_i/\Delta q_i\) over positive credit increments. At a
smooth interior welfare optimum, a nonzero own-action derivative of
credit can defeat every positive prize: the first-order credit gain
dominates the second-order welfare loss. \ledger{26,31}

Raw difference utility
\[
D_i(x)=G(x)-G(x_i^0,x_{-i})
\]
preserves unilateral welfare differences. Hence \(G+\beta D_i\)
preserves welfare-maximizing profiles for \(\beta\geq0\).
Softmax normalization need not preserve that property.
For a two-player coalitional game with singleton values \(v_1,v_2\),
grand-coalition value \(G\), Shapley values \(\phi_i\), and removal
credits \(D_i=G-v_{-i}\),
\[
\phi_1-\phi_2=v_1-v_2=D_1-D_2.
\]
Normalized Shapley and normalized removal credits are identical and
discard the grand-coalition value. Greater comparison accuracy cannot
recover that discarded common component. \ledger{24,31}

If a unilateral change raises the acting agent's share, singleton
comparisons can be held fixed while choosing a compatible welfare
function with an arbitrarily small loss from that deviation.
Sufficiently large baseline production preserves coalition
monotonicity. Thus any fixed positive prize can violate obedience in
some compatible environment. Additional restrictions relating scores
to welfare can prevent the failure; comparison accuracy alone cannot.
Failures of normalized marginal-contribution utilities have close
precedents, so this is not a new general impossibility theorem. See
\url{ada/round3_effective_contests.md}, lines 94--130.
\ledger{24,31,32}

\subsection{A continuous exact-credit construction}

Take \(x_i\in[-1,1]\), \(a,d>0\), and define
\[
\begin{aligned}
G(x)&=nB-d\sum_i x_i^2,\\
v_x(S)&=B|S|+a\sum_{i\in S}x_i,
\qquad S\subsetneq N,\\
v_x(N)&=G(x).
\end{aligned}
\]
Choose \(B>(n-1)a+dn\). Coalition values are monotone, and the
efficient action profile is zero. The exact credits are
\[
\begin{aligned}
\phi_i(x)
&=B+a x_i
-\frac{d\sum_jx_j^2+a\sum_jx_j}{n},\\
D_i(x)
&=B-d\sum_jx_j^2-a\sum_{j\ne i}x_j.
\end{aligned}
\]
Both therefore yield
\[
\operatorname{softmax}(\phi)_i
=\operatorname{softmax}(D)_i
=\frac{e^{a x_i}}{\sum_j e^{a x_j}}=q_i(x).
\]
At zero,
\[
\partial_{x_i}G=0,
\qquad
\partial_{x_i}q_i=\frac{a(n-1)}{n^2}>0.
\]
Every positive effective prize displaces the team optimum.
\ledger{26}

If
\[
\beta a^2<4d,
\qquad
\frac{\beta a(n-1)}{2dn^2}<1,
\]
the effective game has the unique equilibrium
\[
x_i^{\mathrm{eff}}
=\frac{\beta a(n-1)}{2dn^2}.
\]
Own-action strict concavity reduces equilibrium, including boundaries,
to the projected fixed-point equation
\[
x_i=
\operatorname{proj}_{[-1,1]}
\left(\frac{\beta a}{2d}q_i(x)(1-q_i(x))\right).
\]
The sum of absolute derivatives in each row of the unprojected map
is bounded by
\[
\frac{\beta a^2}{d}q_i(1-q_i)|1-2q_i|
\leq\frac{\beta a^2}{4d}<1.
\]
Projection is nonexpansive, so the map is a contraction. Symmetry and
the interior bound identify its fixed point. The welfare loss is
\[
G(0)-G(x^{\mathrm{eff}})
=\frac{\beta^2a^2(n-1)^2}{4dn^3}.
\]
This is an equilibrium calculation for the specified effective game,
not a global convergence result for neural MAPPO or arbitrary
projected-critic learning. \ledger{26,31}

The construction and proof are in
\url{ada/round3_effective_contests.md}, lines 133--202.
The checks in \url{ada/effective-game-003.log}, lines 1--11,
verify 360 GAE cases, 30 Shapley cases, and numerical equilibria for
\(n=2,3,5,10\). These locations retain the corrections made in the
previous note. \ledger{26,34}

\subsection{Sampled clipped-PPO evidence}

These experiments implement sampled trajectories, a shared Bernoulli
actor, complete accounting, GAE, Adam, and clipped PPO. Centralized
tabular critics solve Monte Carlo least-squares regressions on each
rollout before advantage estimation. The aggregate critic sees time
and total action count; the rank-conditioned critic also sees own
action. An explicitly shifted control fits base returns and subtracts
the realized potential. The initial baseline is exact.
\ledger{27,31}

Unless otherwise stated, the protocol uses 512 episodes per update,
ten actor epochs, learning rate \(0.1\), clipping threshold \(0.2\),
\(\gamma=0.99\), \(\lambda=0.95\), and \(\rho=1\), without entropy
bonuses. This is custom fitted-tabular PPO, not reference MAPPO or
MARS-RA. The protocol is in
\url{ada/round3_effective_contests.md}, lines 208--210.
\ledger{27,32,34}

For the small-cost congestion example, ten seeds and 100 updates give
mean harmful-action probability approximately \(0.99966\) with
aggregate shaped critics, versus \(0.00031\) for unshaped,
rank-conditioned, and shifted controls. A separate run reproduces
the harmful direction with advantage normalization. Active clipping
is recorded in \url{ada/clipped-mappo-002.log} and
\url{ada/clipped-mappo-003.log}. \ledger{27}

\paragraph{Delay reverses the direction.}
Use \(a=0.5\) and \(d=0.02\). The predicted initial action-gap sign is
\[
-d+\beta_H\bigl(\sigma(0.5)-1/2\bigr).
\]
At \(H=2\), \(\beta_H=0.05\), and the credit term is approximately
\(0.00612<d\). At \(H=8\), \(\beta_H\approx0.31160\), and the term
is approximately \(0.03816>d\). Only the waiting period changes;
the scores and team-optimal action are unchanged. \ledger{28,31}

After 50 updates over ten seeds, mean harmful-action probability
and standard error are
\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
H&\text{Unshaped}&\text{Aggregate shaped}\\ \hline
2&0.000983\pm0.000034&0.001138\pm0.000040\\
8&0.000983\pm0.000034&0.998462\pm0.000030
\end{array}
\]
Rank-conditioned and shifted controls match the unshaped runs
seed by seed. Aggregate-shaped clipped fractions average approximately
\(1.20\%\) and \(0.98\%\), respectively. Source logs are
\url{ada/clipped-mappo-008.log} and
\url{ada/clipped-mappo-009.log}; summaries appear in
\url{ada/round3-summary-001.log}, lines 9--17.
\ledger{28,31}

\paragraph{Removing the residual can remove useful guidance.}
A six-agent sparse task pays one only if every agent chooses action
one. Initial action probability is \(0.1\), giving success probability
\(10^{-6}\). There are eight transitions and no later decisions.
Progress scores \(c_i=x_i\) are exact Shapley values of
\[
v_x^{\mathrm{progress}}(S)=\sum_{i\in S}x_i.
\]
They are not Shapley values of the terminal unanimity reward.
\ledger{29}

After 100 updates over ten seeds, success probabilities are
\[
\begin{array}{l|r}
\text{Condition}&\text{Mean}\ \pm\ \text{standard error}\\ \hline
\text{Unshaped}&0.299759\pm0.152630\\
\text{Progress shares, aggregate critic}&0.999568\pm0.000016\\
\text{Progress shares, rank-conditioned}&0.299759\pm0.152630\\
\text{Progress shares, shifted}&0.299759\pm0.152630
\end{array}
\]
All aggregate-shaped seeds exceed \(80\%\) success at update seven;
only three unshaped seeds reach that level during the run.
With true Shapley values of the terminal unanimity game, shares are
uniform on failure and success, and every condition matches the
unshaped control. A separate threshold task with true Shapley credit
showed only a very small benefit. \ledger{27,29}

These examples establish useful and harmful residual effects, not
their necessary inseparability. The progress task passes the effective
obedience test; the congestion task fails it. Distinguishing them
requires information relating credit to welfare. See
\url{ada/round3_effective_contests.md}, lines 233--252,
and \url{ada/round3-summary-001.log}, lines 1--8.
\ledger{29,31,32}

\paragraph{Numerical correction.}
The first sparse-game run,
\url{ada/clipped-mappo-004.log}, showed divergence between
mathematically identical controls. Tiny cancellation errors interacted
with clipping and rare successes. The corrected implementation uses
the algebraically equivalent critic-residual expression, removes only
residual roundoff below \(10^{-12}\), and checks agreement with direct
TD arithmetic within \(10^{-11}\). Supported sparse-game results use
\url{ada/clipped-mappo-007.log}; earlier logs remain preserved.
This is a numerical correction, not a reward intervention.
See \url{ada/round3_effective_contests.md}, lines 254--256.
\ledger{30}

\section{Results retained from the fourth round}

\subsection{The critic residual is an unrepresented potential component}

Fix a policy, finite horizon, complete accounting, cached predecision
potentials, and terminal potential zero. If \(Y_t\) is the Monte Carlo
return from time \(t\), then pathwise
\[
\widetilde Y_{i,t}=Y_t-\rho\psi_{i,t}.
\]
Fit both targets under the same trajectory distribution and in the
same closed linear critic space \(\mathcal H_t\). Let \(\Pi_t\) be
its weighted \(L^2\) orthogonal projection:
\[
\widehat V_t=\Pi_tY_t,
\qquad
\widehat{\widetilde V}_{i,t}=\Pi_t\widetilde Y_{i,t}.
\]
Linearity gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\widehat{\widetilde V}_{i,t}-\widehat V_t
&=-\rho\Pi_t\psi_{i,t},\\
e_{i,t}
&=\widehat{\widetilde V}_{i,t}-\widehat V_t+\rho\psi_{i,t}\\
&=\rho(I-\Pi_t)\psi_{i,t}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus the residual is exactly the component of the potential that the
critic cannot represent. \ledger{36}

The identity also holds for empirical linear least squares with the
same design matrix and weights, and for population regression over
all functions of fixed features, where \(\Pi_t\) is conditional
expectation. It requires neither an exact base value nor
exchangeable policies nor the absence of later decisions.
It does require a common projection problem. Finite nonlinear
networks, TD or lambda-return fitting, and separately learned
representations do not generally satisfy this exact projection
identity. In those cases \(e\) must be measured from its definition.
See \url{ada/round4_critic_information.md}, lines 25--53.
\ledger{36,44}

For a complete terminal rollout, the earlier GAE identity becomes
\[
\widetilde A_{i,t}-A_{i,t}
=-e_{i,t}
+\gamma(1-\lambda)
\sum_{k=1}^{T-t-1}(\gamma\lambda)^{k-1}e_{i,t+k}.
\]
Let
\[
S_{i,t}=\nabla\log\pi_i(a_{i,t}\mid h_{i,t}).
\]
With frozen critics and the current residual fixed before the current
action, its expected score-function contribution vanishes under the
predecision conditioning used by the policy. Hence
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\mathbb E\!\left[
S_{i,t}(\widetilde A_{i,t}-A_{i,t})
\right]\\
&\qquad=
\gamma(1-\lambda)
\sum_{k=1}^{T-t-1}(\gamma\lambda)^{k-1}
\mathbb E[S_{i,t}e_{i,t+k}].
\end{aligned}
\]
This extends the update analysis to repeated decisions and unequal
policies under the stated conditioning. Future residual action
covariance, rather than residual mean-squared error alone, determines
the expected distortion. \ledger{36,37}

At a frozen policy and critic, this contribution has the algebraic
form of a short-horizon heuristic with reward
\(\gamma(1-\lambda)e_{i,t+1}\) and discount \(\gamma\lambda\).
It is generally not the gradient of a single policy-independent game:
the projection distribution changes with the policy, actor occupancy
weights need not match the shortened discount, and clipping changes
later PPO steps. The earlier single-decision effective contest remains
a special case. See \url{ada/round4_critic_information.md},
lines 55--82. Exact enumeration with three decisions and unequal,
time-varying policies gives error below \(8\times10^{-16}\) in
\url{ada/projection-queries-001.log}, line 1.
\ledger{36,37}

\subsection{Which residual incentives can added features select?}

Fix a base linear critic space \(\mathcal H_0\), policy distribution,
and potential. Write
\[
z=(I-\Pi_0)\psi.
\]

\begin{claim}
If arbitrary required features are available, an unscaled residual
\(r\) can be obtained by enlarging the critic space
\(\mathcal H\supseteq\mathcal H_0\) exactly when
\[
r\perp\mathcal H_0,
\qquad
\langle r,z-r\rangle=0.
\]
\end{claim}

Necessity follows because a projection residual is orthogonal to both
the original space and the captured component. For sufficiency choose
\[
\mathcal H=\mathcal H_0+\operatorname{span}\{z-r\}.
\]
The conditions make the projection of \(z\) onto the added span equal
to \(z-r\), leaving residual \(r\). Equivalently,
\[
\|r-z/2\|^2=\|z\|^2/4
\quad\text{within }\mathcal H_0^\perp.
\]
Consequently, fractional attenuation \(r=\alpha z\), with
\(0<\alpha<1\), is not achievable by a subspace projection alone unless
\(z=0\). Regularization, incomplete fitting, or changing \(\rho\) can
produce attenuation through different mechanisms. \ledger{39,40}

For multiple output potentials using one common feature space, put
the residualized columns in \(Z\), desired residuals in \(R\), and
positive state weights in \(W\). The necessary and sufficient
conditions are
\[
R\perp\mathcal H_0,
\qquad
R^{\mathsf T}W(Z-R)=0.
\]
Every retained output must be orthogonal to every captured output;
checking only diagonal inner products is insufficient. Adding the
span of the columns of \(Z-R\) proves sufficiency. Under a common
space, zero-sum centered potentials also retain zero-sum residuals.
\ledger{40}

Restricted feature access additionally constrains the captured columns
to the available span. If the base value is not represented in
\(\mathcal H_0\), enlargement can change the base critic as well,
creating another update channel. These are instrument restrictions
on critic-based design, not Q's unrestricted reward design.
The argument is elementary projection geometry, not a new general
mathematical theorem. See
\url{ada/round4_critic_information.md}, lines 84--128.
Two hundred reconstruction checks pass with error below
\(6\times10^{-15}\) in \url{ada/projection-design-001.log},
line 6. \ledger{39,40,44}

\paragraph{Separation needs information and orthogonality.}
If \(z=p+h\), where \(p\) is desired progress and \(h\) is harmful,
retaining \(p\) exactly while capturing \(h\) requires \(p\perp h\)
after residualizing against the base features. For multiple outputs
the corresponding cross-Gram matrix must vanish. For unit-norm
scalar components with correlation \(\kappa\), adding the harmful
feature gives
\[
r=p-\kappa h,
\qquad
\langle p,r\rangle=1-\kappa^2.
\]
Capturing harm therefore removes correlated desired signal too.
Independent components can be separated; identical components cannot.
A policy change can alter the relevant correlation. \ledger{37,40}

The designer must identify or estimate the harmful component, its
action effects, and the feature distribution. Accurate comparisons
alone provide none of these labels. The characterization does not
overcome the earlier comparison-only indistinguishability limitation.
See \url{ada/round4_critic_information.md}, lines 130--144,
and \url{ada/projection-design-001.log}, lines 1--5.
\ledger{37,40,44}

\subsection{Selective guidance in a repeated mixed task}

Six agents make productive choices \(p_{i,t}\) and harmful display
choices \(h_{i,t}\), both binary, at three decision dates. A stage
succeeds only if every productive choice equals one. Terminal welfare
is average stage success minus \(0.006\) times the display count per
stage, followed by eight additional waiting transitions before
payment. At each stage the oracle supplies
\[
q_{i,t}
=\tfrac12\operatorname{softmax}(p_t)_i
+\tfrac12\operatorname{softmax}(h_t)_i.
\]
These positive shares are exactly representable by Bradley--Terry
scores \(\log q_{i,t}\). They deliberately mix productive progress
and harmful display; they are not terminal-task Shapley values.
The potential accumulates share deviations from uniform, remains
fixed during the delay, and is zero at termination, preserving
complete-return preferences. \ledger{37,41}

The base value is exact. Projected critics absorb neither component,
both components, only display, or only progress. Exchangeable
independent policies and permutation-invariant aggregate progress
features permit the intended separation. The productive and display
channels are independent by construction. These assumptions establish
the possibility of separation with supplied knowledge, not a general
method for discovering it. \ledger{37,41,46}

Sampled clipped PPO uses separate progress and display logits,
three actual decisions, 512 episodes per update, ten actor epochs,
Adam, 120 updates, and five seeds. Mean final probabilities and
standard errors are
\[
\begin{array}{l|cc}
\text{Captured component}
&\text{Stage success}&\text{Harmful choice}\\ \hline
\text{Neither}
&0.999350\pm0.000014&0.998539\pm0.000167\\
\text{Display only}
&0.999352\pm0.000014&0.004145\pm0.000730\\
\text{Both}
&0.598587\pm0.244373&0.000527\pm0.000079\\
\text{Progress only}
&0.599596\pm0.244784&0.999037\pm0.000045
\end{array}
\]
Corresponding mean welfare is \(0.963402\), \(0.999203\),
\(0.598568\), and \(0.563630\), respectively.
With only display absorbed, all five seeds exceed \(80\%\) success
at update nine. With both components absorbed, two seeds never reach
that level during the run. \ledger{41}

This establishes that guidance and harm need not be removed together.
Its critics are exact projections, not neural fits. Protocol and
results are in \url{ada/round4_critic_information.md},
lines 146--172; the summary is in
\url{ada/round4-summary-001.log}, lines 12--15.
Exact conditional-projection and action-covariance checks appear in
\url{ada/projection-queries-001.log}, line 2.
The initial selective-run dependency failure supplies no result.
\ledger{37,41,44}

\subsection{Neural tests with repeated decisions and nonshared actors}

The neural probe has three agents and three genuine decisions.
Choosing one increments the agent's cumulative display count \(x_i\).
The terminal reward and nonterminal potential are
\[
G=1-0.02\sum_i x_i,
\qquad
\psi_i=\operatorname{softmax}(0.5x)_i.
\]
The team-optimal policy always chooses zero. This deterministic score
oracle has no comparison noise. The probe does not define a coalition
game or claim terminal-task Shapley values; the earlier exact-Shapley
construction is a separate result. \ledger{38,42}

Each agent has its own two-layer tanh actor, observes time and its own
count, and starts at a different action probability. The shared
two-layer critic receives either time and total count, those features
plus own count, or global ordered counts plus agent identity. An
additional diagnostic supplies the realized oracle share. Actors have
width 16; critics have width 32.

The custom centralized-critic PPO uses rollout GAE, clipped actor
updates, and squared-error lambda-return fitting. It uses 512 episodes
per update, 120 updates, eight actor and critic epochs, Adam learning
rate \(0.003\), \(\gamma=0.99\), \(\lambda=0.95\), and clip ratio
\(0.2\). These are eight full-batch critic steps per update. By
comparison, P's batch size 10240, minibatch size 512, and ten epochs
imply approximately 200 minibatch steps. The neural probe's unshaped
and explicitly shifted controls used global features, including when
the ordinary shaped condition had aggregate features. The later
visual round addresses both limitations. \ledger{42,46,52}

The neural probe omits recurrence, entropy bonuses, value clipping,
environment randomization, and minibatch shuffling. Membership is
fixed. Main runs omit advantage normalization; follow-ups add it.
This is neither reference MAPPO nor MARS-RA. See
\url{ada/round4_critic_information.md}, lines 174--201,
and \url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 75--85.
\ledger{38,42,44,46}

Dynamic programming computes the exact current-policy base value
only for diagnostics:
\[
e^{\mathrm{true}}_{i,t}
=\widehat{\widetilde V}_{i,t}-V^\pi_t+\rho\psi_{i,t}.
\]
This includes all shaped-critic error, not just a projection residual.
Every rollout is checked for telescoping and the GAE decomposition.
Logged action covariances use Bernoulli logit scores and represent
an interpretable component of the neural update, not its whole
parameter-gradient vector. \ledger{38,42}

Across three seeds, mean harmful-action frequency over the last
20 updates, with standard error, is
\[
\begin{array}{l|cc}
\text{Critic}&\text{No added delay}&\text{Eight waiting steps}\\ \hline
\text{Unshaped, global}
&0.000698\pm0.000058&0.000600\pm0.000050\\
\text{Shaped, aggregate}
&0.001107\pm0.000157&0.999045\pm0.000060\\
\text{Shaped, own count}
&0.000720\pm0.000056&0.001396\pm0.000264\\
\text{Shaped, global and identity}
&0.001031\pm0.000154&0.998933\pm0.000044
\end{array}
\]
Explicitly shifted controls match unshaped controls seed by seed.
For the delayed aggregate critic, mean residual action covariance is
\(4.16\times10^{-5}\), exceeding the oppositely signed exact-base
covariance \(-1.74\times10^{-5}\). With own-count inputs, the residual
covariance is approximately \(2.39\times10^{-7}\), much smaller than
the negative task term. These signs support the mechanism, without
proving equilibrium or convergence. See
\url{ada/round4_critic_information.md}, lines 203--217,
and \url{ada/round4-summary-001.log}, lines 1--11.
\ledger{42}

\paragraph{Absorption limits the failure.}
The global-plus-identity network has sufficient information in
principle but must learn the interaction between identity and count.
Its failure at the original budget is therefore an optimization or
coverage limitation, not a missing-information theorem.
Single-seed delayed diagnostics give harmful frequency approximately
\(0.00505\) after adding the share input and \(0.00133\) after
increasing critic epochs from eight to forty. Monte Carlo targets
with eight epochs do not repair that seed, giving \(0.99889\);
forty epochs with Monte Carlo targets give \(0.00137\).
\ledger{38,42}

Across three seeds, advantage normalization preserves the contrast:
delayed aggregate frequency is \(0.999884\pm0.000022\), versus
\(0.000101\pm0.000070\) with own-count inputs. Aligning initial shaped
predictions with the uniform potential also preserves it:
\(0.999226\pm0.000057\) versus \(0.000940\pm0.000098\).
Aligned global-plus-identity networks remain near \(0.998998\).
Thus the omitted initial constant adjustment does not explain the
main contrast. See \url{ada/round4_critic_information.md},
lines 219--234, and \url{ada/round4-followups-001.log},
lines 1--13. \ledger{42}

A sufficiently trained informative critic can absorb the comparative
signal. These finite runs establish neither persistence under unlimited
training nor an error in P's architecture. The initial neural
shape-error run was corrected before the successful experiments and
does not supply evidence. \ledger{38,42,44}

\subsection{Query count changes noise, and sometimes the mean}

For two agents with \(K\) independent binary comparisons, write
\(W\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(K,p)\). When both outcomes occur,
the normalized unregularized Bradley--Terry MLE is
\[
\widehat q_1=W/K.
\]
On a sweep the score difference diverges, although its normalized
limit remains zero or one. With this explicit boundary convention,
\[
\mathbb E[\widehat q_1]=p,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat q_1)=\frac{p(1-p)}{K}.
\]
More queries leave the conditional mean effective prize unchanged
while reducing sampling noise. Under independent query batches, the
query contribution to update variance scales as \(\rho^2/K\), with
temporal weights supplied by the GAE identity. It can reduce
uncertainty around harmful as well as useful updates. \ledger{43}

For \(p_0=1/2\), \(p_1=\sigma(0.5)\), action cost \(0.02\), and
effective prize \(\beta\), the tested mean score update is
\[
\tfrac14[-0.02+\beta(p_1-p_0)].
\]
It is negative for \(\beta=0.05\) and positive for
\(\beta=0.3116\) at every tested query count from 1 to 64.
Only the query variance shrinks. Exact enumeration is recorded in
\url{ada/projection-queries-001.log}, lines 3--14.
\ledger{43}

Connected comparisons alone do not guarantee a finite score MLE.
At equal two-agent scores, the sweep probability is \(2^{1-K}\);
with two binary comparisons it is one half despite connectivity.
P's proof mentions regularization, but its displayed estimator does
not specify it. Gauge choice, score-range control, and the treatment
of divergent estimates matter for its recovery claim. This is the
ordinary separation problem, not a new theorem or a verified
implementation defect. \ledger{34,43}

A symmetric pseudocount \(\delta>0\) instead gives
\[
\mathbb E[\widehat q_1]
=\frac{Kp+\delta}{K+2\delta},
\]
so count changes shrinkage as well as variance.
For three agents the fitted share is nonlinear. Exact enumeration
with half-win pseudocounts and true scores \((0.5,0,-0.5)\)
gives top-agent mean share \(0.41730\) at one query per pair and
\(0.49696\) at sixteen, approaching the true \(0.50648\).
The mean fitted share differs from fitting the mean counts.
In this smoothed example, top-share variance rises slightly from
one to two queries before declining. See
\url{ada/three-agent-queries-001.log}, lines 1--5.
\ledger{43}

These are estimator and variance channels; they do not explain P's
empirical accuracy-query tradeoff or predict an optimal \(\rho\),
including its reported favorable range \(\{0.1,0.5,1\}\).
Such a prediction needs P's actual estimator, critic learning,
and the relationship between visual credit and task progress.
The general explanation sought by the verifier was not obtained.
See \url{ada/round4_critic_information.md}, lines 236--272.
\ledger{43,44}

\section{Appended findings from the fifth round}

\subsection{An explicitly shifted critic is an exact intervention}

Fix actor information, cached predecision potentials, terminal-zero
complete accounting, the discount, and a base critic parameterization.
Define
\[
\widetilde V_\phi(h_t)=V_\phi(h_t)-\rho\psi_t.
\]
Substitution into the TD residual cancels the potential samplewise.
Therefore GAE advantages are identical for every trace parameter,
including after identical advantage normalization. Their lambda-return
targets satisfy
\[
\widetilde A_t+\widetilde V_\phi(h_t)
=A_t+V_\phi(h_t)-\rho\psi_t.
\]
Consequently the squared-error critic loss also agrees:
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\left[
\widetilde V_\phi(h_t)
-\bigl(\widetilde A_t+\widetilde V_{\mathrm{old}}(h_t)\bigr)
\right]^2\\
&\qquad=
\left[
V_\phi(h_t)-\bigl(A_t+V_{\mathrm{old}}(h_t)\bigr)
\right]^2,
\end{aligned}
\]
where both target predictions use the corresponding fixed rollout
parameters and the same potential shift. \ledger{47,57}

With identical initialization, optimizer state, minibatches, and actor
inputs, and no gradient through the potential, the coupled
parameterizations have identical actor and critic parameter paths.
This does not require exact values or linear critics. It is an
intervention imposed on the parameterization, not a claim that
independently fitted neural critics will discover the relation.
Different normalization rules, actor information, or gradients through
the comparator can invalidate the coupling. See
\url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 13--46.
\ledger{48,57}

For ordinary shaped critics, the comparison residual is measured
against a shadow base critic fitted on the same policy's rollouts:
\[
e_{i,t}
=\widehat{\widetilde V}_{i,t}
-\widehat V_{i,t}+\rho\psi_{i,t}.
\]
It is not formed by subtracting critics from separate runs with
different policies. The shadow critic is diagnostic; the ordinary
condition's actor uses its ordinary shaped GAE. The equivalent
residual expression is checked against direct shaped TD arithmetic.
This separates a pathwise identity from cross-run intervention
evidence. \ledger{53,56,57}

\subsection{Visual task, comparisons, and matched controls}

The new task is a custom \(5\times5\) visual gridworld with two robots,
a central wall with a single opening, and separate goals on the far
side. Simultaneous collisions and swaps block moves. There are
20 decision dates and five actions: stay, up, down, left, and right.
A shared sparse reward of one is paid at the fixed terminal date if
both robots reach their goals. The delay condition adds eight
no-action transitions before paying the same reward. Membership is
fixed. \ledger{47,50,57}

Actors are separate small CNNs. Each observes symbolic image planes
for walls, itself, its teammate, and both goals, plus time.
These are fully observed symbolic pixels, not photorealistic or
egocentric observations. The shared critic receives the state image
in own-relative coordinates, time, and agent identity. A second
input class also receives the cached own comparison share.
Within each input class, the study compares unshaped learning,
explicitly shifted shaping, and ordinary shaping. Thus critic input
access is matched across controls. This is custom visual CTDE PPO,
not XuanCe, reference MAPPO, or MARS-RA. See
\url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 48--85.
\ledger{50,57}

The synthetic comparator uses static shortest-path distance to the
agent's goal, ignoring the other robot. Its correct label is win,
loss, or tie. It returns the correct label with probability \(a\)
and each other label with probability \((1-a)/2\).
The primary accuracies are \(a=0.43\) and \(a=0.83\).
They span P's reported numerical range but do not model its LMM
errors, contribution semantics, partial observations, or correlated
judgments. \ledger{50}

At each state, four independent comparisons are cached. A win counts
one, a tie one half, and a loss zero. If their sum is \(W_t\),
\[
\widehat q_{1,t}=\frac{W_t+1/2}{K+1},
\qquad
\widehat q_{2,t}=1-\widehat q_{1,t},
\qquad K=4.
\]
This is an explicitly smoothed two-agent Bradley--Terry share, not a
claim about P's unspecified optimizer. Initial shares are \(1/2\)
and terminal potentials are zero. Query noise and action sampling
use separate random streams. Across diverging policies, controls
share the state-conditional generator and random draws; their visited
states, and hence realized judgments, need not remain identical.
\ledger{50,57}

\paragraph{Budget and protocol corrections.}
The main protocol uses 128 full episodes per update, 80 updates,
ten shuffled epochs, minibatch size 256, Adam at \(0.001\),
discount \(0.99\), GAE \(0.95\), clipping ratio \(0.2\), entropy
coefficient \(0.01\), and gradient norm clipping at \(0.5\).
At zero delay, the critic sees 5120 agent transitions and takes
200 minibatch steps per update; each actor participates in 100
joint-minibatch steps. Delay increases critic steps to 280 while
actor steps remain 100. The isolated low-critic-budget condition
uses two critic epochs, or 40 steps, while retaining ten actor epochs.
\ledger{52,54,57}

The main critic step count matches the count implied by P's
hyperparameter table, but minibatches contain half as many samples,
and the network and task are much smaller. This is not equal total
compute. Delay also increases critic fitting, so the delay experiment
does not hold that count constant. The implementation omits
recurrence, value clipping, value normalization, adaptive learning
rates, action repeat, dynamic participants, and LMM inference.
See \url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 75--85.
\ledger{52,57}

Initial binary-label runs made accuracy \(0.43\) below chance and
therefore were not comparable to P's three-way accuracy. They remain
sensitivity checks; the primary runs use three labels.
Earlier low-budget runs changed actor and critic epochs together.
Their failures cannot isolate critic fitting. The corrected
critic-budget comparison changes only critic epochs.
Neither correction overwrites the earlier logs.
\ledger{50,52,54}

\subsection{Visual results and negative generalization evidence}

The primary protocol comprises 66 runs: 36 main, 18 delayed, and
12 isolated critic-budget runs. Another 78 runs remain binary-label
or combined-budget sensitivity checks. Noise-decomposition repetitions
are not extra independent seeds. All available explicit-shift controls
match their unshaped controls on logged metrics, including critic
losses and learning summaries. Complete-return and GAE checks passed.
See \url{ada/visual-summary-final-001.log}, line 49, and
\url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 190--217.
\ledger{57}

The following table reports training success averaged over the
80-update learning curve, with standard errors across three seeds.
Here P denotes pixel inputs and S denotes pixels plus the comparison
share. The shifted control equals the input-matched base exactly.
\[
\begin{array}{llc|cc}
\text{Condition}&a&\text{Input}
&\text{Base/shifted}&\text{Ordinary}\\ \hline
\text{Main}&0.43&\mathrm P
&0.7303\pm0.0130&0.4439\pm0.2278\\
\text{Main}&0.43&\mathrm S
&0.8299\pm0.0287&0.5279\pm0.2653\\
\text{Main}&0.83&\mathrm P
&0.7303\pm0.0130&0.4267\pm0.2402\\
\text{Main}&0.83&\mathrm S
&0.8322\pm0.0261&0.8523\pm0.0081\\
\text{Delay}&0.83&\mathrm P
&0.7481\pm0.0104&0.6762\pm0.1159\\
\text{Delay}&0.83&\mathrm S
&0.8410\pm0.0244&0.8951\pm0.0024\\
\text{Low critic}&0.83&\mathrm P
&0.7414\pm0.0051&0.6975\pm0.0455\\
\text{Low critic}&0.83&\mathrm S
&0.8344\pm0.0233&0.8390\pm0.0172
\end{array}
\]
For ordinary shaping, final success averaged over the last ten
training updates and late residual RMS are
\[
\begin{array}{llc|cc}
\text{Condition}&a&\text{Input}
&\text{Final success}&\text{Residual RMS}\\ \hline
\text{Main}&0.43&\mathrm P&0.6643\pm0.3322&0.1562\\
\text{Main}&0.43&\mathrm S&0.6656\pm0.3328&0.0073\\
\text{Main}&0.83&\mathrm P&0.6625\pm0.3313&0.1060\\
\text{Main}&0.83&\mathrm S&0.9987\pm0.0009&0.0053\\
\text{Delay}&0.83&\mathrm P&0.9961\pm0.0012&0.0963\\
\text{Delay}&0.83&\mathrm S&0.9990\pm0.0007&0.0048\\
\text{Low critic}&0.83&\mathrm P&0.9938\pm0.0024&0.1035\\
\text{Low critic}&0.83&\mathrm S&0.9945\pm0.0008&0.0077
\end{array}
\]
All base/shifted final success means exceed \(0.997\).
These are training-rollout measurements, not held-out deployment
evaluations or layout-generalization tests. The table is supported by
\url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 194--205, and
\url{ada/visual-final-table-001.log}, lines 1--24.
\ledger{54,56,57}

The main ordinary pixel-only condition has a zero-success seed at
each accuracy, producing large uncertainty. Higher comparison accuracy
does not monotonically improve its mean learning average in this
sample. With share inputs, high-accuracy ordinary shaping succeeds
in all seeds and has a modest positive mean difference from its
input-matched base. The delayed share-input condition also has a
positive mean difference. These observations support possible transient
guidance, not a benchmark-wide benefit. \ledger{55,57}

Comparison features help the unshaped critic too: its zero-delay
learning average rises from approximately \(0.73\) with pixels to
\(0.83\) with share inputs. Input matching is therefore necessary to
separate critic information from reward shaping. Ordinary share-input
critics absorb almost all the potential offset late in training;
small residuals and changed early trajectories can still accompany
different learning curves. \ledger{57}

Most importantly, the visual task does not reproduce the earlier
delay collapse. All delayed high-accuracy ordinary conditions learn,
with or without share inputs. All isolated low-critic-budget
high-accuracy conditions also learn. The earlier failures under a
combined actor-and-critic budget reduction are not evidence that low
critic budget alone causes failure. These outcomes limit extrapolation
from the constructed congestion examples without invalidating them.
\ledger{52,54,56,57}

\subsection{Query noise need not create a mean rank contest}

For a critic without comparison outputs, decompose the potential as
\[
\psi_t=\mu(s_t)+\varepsilon_t,
\qquad
\mathbb E[\varepsilon_t\mid s_t]=0.
\]
In this visual protocol the actor does not observe query output,
the comparator does not choose environment actions, and query draws
are independent across time conditional on visited states. If the
base value and conditional-mean shaped value are fitted exactly,
then \(e_t=\rho\varepsilon_t\). Iterated conditioning yields
\[
\mathbb E[S_{i,t}e_{i,t+k}]=0.
\]
Thus a nonzero residual RMS can coexist with zero expected initial
score-function distortion. It can still increase finite-sample
variance and alter clipping, normalization, and subsequent learning.
See \url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 93--117.
\ledger{53}

The synthetic generator permits a separate measurement of
\[
\begin{aligned}
e_t^{\mathrm{mean}}
&=\widehat{\widetilde V}_t-\widehat V_t+\rho\mu(s_t),\\
e_t^{\mathrm{noise}}
&=\rho\bigl(\psi_t-\mu(s_t)\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]
For the three high-accuracy pixel-only seeds, late total residual RMS
is approximately \(0.103,0.103,0.113\); query-noise RMS alone is
\(0.100,0.099,0.112\), and mean-signal fitting error is
\(0.021,0.026,0.014\). Much of the measured residual is therefore
unpredictable query noise. These are diagnostic repetitions of the
same primary seeds, not new trials. See
\url{ada/round5-audit-and-noise-summary-001.log},
lines 13--15. \ledger{53,55}

The failing seed has essentially no task-gradient signal because
successful feedback is absent. A raw RMS threshold would conflate
query noise, fitting error, and behavioral consequences. For a critic
that receives the realized share, predictions also depend on the
query draw, so this simple state-only decomposition is not applied.
No persistent mean rank contest is established in the visual task.
\ledger{53,55,58}

\subsection{A task-return audit identifies only a local effect}

Let \(J(\theta)\) be the discounted task objective,
\(g=\nabla J(\theta)\), and \(d\) a fixed proposed auxiliary direction
selected before drawing an independent validation batch. With a
preaction baseline, an unbiased trajectory estimate is
\[
\widehat g(\tau)=
\sum_{i,t}\gamma^t
\nabla_\theta\log\pi_i(a_{i,t}\mid h_{i,t})
\bigl(Y_t-b_{i,t}\bigr).
\]
The sample mean of
\[
X(\tau)=d^{\mathsf T}\widehat g(\tau)
\]
estimates \(g^{\mathsf T}d\) without labels identifying harmful
comparison components. Selection on the same data requires additional
correction; optimizer preconditioning also requires testing the
direction actually applied. \ledger{49,51}

Comparing shaped GAE with Monte Carlo returns detects total critic
and GAE bias. To isolate comparative shaping, compare shaped and base
GAE on common rollouts, or subtract the base-GAE discrepancy.
Otherwise ordinary critic bias is attributed to comparisons.
\ledger{49,51}

A negative \(g^{\mathsf T}d\) means that the auxiliary component
opposes immediate task improvement. It does not imply that the
combined update is harmful. For an ordinary gradient step on \(g+d\),
first-order improvement requires
\[
\|g\|^2+g^{\mathsf T}d>0.
\]
Finite steps require curvature control or direct evaluation.
At a stationary point the first-order statistic can be zero even
when every nonzero nearby step lowers the objective. This audit
does not establish Q's global deviation inequalities. See
\url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 119--139.
\ledger{49,51}

\paragraph{Sparse feedback imposes an information limit.}
Consider two environments with identical action and observation
processes and identical comparisons. An independent informative reward
event occurs with probability \(p\) per episode. On that event, one
environment rewards action one and the other rewards action zero;
outside it both pay zero. Their task gradients have opposite signs.

Couple actions, queries, and event indicators. Unless an informative
event occurs, the entire audit data agree in both environments.
For \(N\) episodes this event has probability \((1-p)^N\).
Every binary sign test therefore has worst-case error at least
\[
\frac12(1-p)^N.
\]
At \(p=0.001\) and \(N=128\), the bound is approximately \(0.4399\).
Abstention avoids unwarranted certification but supplies no missing
information. More comparisons cannot reduce this bound because their
distribution is identical in both environments. This is an elementary
indistinguishability argument, not a new statistical theorem.
See \url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 141--151.
\ledger{49,51}

\subsection{Correlated-component audit and confidence correction}

The audit simulation uses binary productive and display choices
\((P,H)\), marginal probabilities \(1/2\), and correlations
\(\kappa\in\{0,0.6,0.95\}\). A passive peer gets the complementary
share. The acting agent's comparison share and task reward are
\[
q=\sigma(P+2H),
\qquad
R=Z(P-cH),
\]
where \(Z\) is an independent event indicator, and
\(c\in\{0.25,2\}\). Thus productive and harmful components can be
strongly correlated, unlike the constructed selective-guidance task.
For the four-category policy with probabilities \(\mu_j\), the
comparative direction is supplied exactly as
\[
d_j=\beta\mu_j\bigl(q_j-\mathbb E[q]\bigr),
\qquad \beta=0.05.
\]
Exact enumeration verifies the true directional derivative.
Independent return-only estimates use validation sizes
128, 1024, and 8192, with 3000 trials per configuration.
This is a fixed-policy diagnostic, not an online learning algorithm.
\ledger{49,51}

The final confidence calculation uses the two-sided empirical
Bernstein bound of Maurer and Pontil, rescaled to
\(X\in[-M,M]\). With sample variance \(s^2\),
\(L=\log(4/\delta)\), and \(N>1\), its radius is
\[
\sqrt{\frac{2s^2L}{N}}
+\frac{14ML}{3(N-1)}.
\]
The corrected calculation is in \url{ada/return-audit-002.log}.
Log 001 is superseded for certification rates but remains preserved.
This bound applies to each fixed independent validation experiment,
not repeated adaptive monitoring without correction.
See \url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 153--176.
\ledger{51}

At cost \(2\) with dense feedback, the conservative audit identifies
the harmful direction in all 3000 trials with \(N=128\), at both
correlation zero and \(0.95\). At event probability \(0.001\) and
\(N=128\), approximately \(88\%\) of batches contain no informative
event and no trial certifies harm. Even at \(N=8192\), the tested
rare-feedback configurations do not certify harm, although the
unqualified sign estimate is correct in approximately \(99.8\%\)
of trials at correlation zero and \(98.5\%\) at \(0.95\).
The certificate's conservatism is distinct from the information
limit applying to every test. Summary evidence appears in
\url{ada/round5-audit-and-noise-summary-001.log}, lines 1--12.
\ledger{51}

No adaptive audit was trained to gate the visual PPO updates.
Visual diagnostics include categorical-score covariance and, every
fifth update, discounted CNN parameter-gradient calculations.
A split-rollout inner product removes same-sample inner-product bias
at a fixed policy with frozen critics. These remain noisy raw
score-function diagnostics. PPO normalizes advantages, omits the
extra discounted-occupancy factor in its training surrogate, clips
later ratios, and uses Adam; its eventual displacement is different.
\ledger{51,56}

The time-averaged alignment diagnostic does not order visual learning
performance. It is positive in the main pixel-only conditions despite
their worse learning averages, and slightly negative in the main
high-accuracy share-input condition despite its positive mean learning
difference. This does not refute the directional-derivative identity.
It rules out treating the measured statistic as an established
learning-curve predictor or safety certificate. See
\url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 87--91 and 213--215.
\ledger{56,57}

\section{Objections and their current status}

\begin{objection}
The participation correction establishes a novel defect in P.
\end{objection}

The identity and counterexample remain valid conditionally, but
neither novelty nor attribution follows. Public backbones distinguish
return accounting from loss masking, P's adapter remains unverified,
and episodic boundary effects are prior work. No implementation-bug
claim survives. \ledger{10,12,14,19,22,34}

\begin{objection}
Accurate comparisons and complete-return invariance prevent harmful
learning incentives.
\end{objection}

They preserve the specified complete-return preferences, not all
approximate updates. Exact Shapley scores can produce a residual
contest under a fully fitted critic missing individual-credit
information. Sampled clipping and custom neural repeated-decision
tests preserve a delay-dependent harmful direction. The neural probe
uses a separate deterministic oracle and does not itself establish
terminal Shapley attribution. None of these results changes the
equilibrium of the correctly shaped return game or establishes a
failure of P. \ledger{15,17,18,28,34,42}

\begin{objection}
The analysis applies only to exchangeable actors making one decision.
\end{objection}

That restriction remains relevant to the particular fitted-aggregate
effective-game formula. The projection and covariance identities
extend to repeated decisions and unequal policies under their common
regression and conditioning assumptions. The neural probe also uses
repeated decisions and nonshared actors. Neither extension supplies
a policy-independent effective game for arbitrary evolving critics
and policies. \ledger{31,36,37,42}

\begin{objection}
A rank-conditioned critic solves the problem, or removing harm must
remove useful guidance.
\end{objection}

Complete absorption removes the demonstrated update distortion and
can remove useful progress guidance. Selective absorption retains
guidance in the constructed mixed task, but requires the supplied
decomposition and orthogonality. The feature characterization explains
why arbitrary corrections cannot be implemented through critic
features alone. The later return audit avoids decomposition labels
only for a local diagnostic, with sufficient task feedback.
\ledger{29,37,40,41,49,51}

\begin{objection}
The neural and visual residuals establish a permanent rank contest.
\end{objection}

The aggregate neural critic has an explicit information restriction.
Global inputs plus identity are sufficient in principle, and more
fitting or direct share inputs repair the tested seed. The visual
experiment further limits the interpretation: delayed and isolated
low-critic-budget high-accuracy runs succeed, and pixel-only residual
RMS is largely explained by query noise. These outcomes preserve the
conditional counterexamples but do not establish a persistent mean
contest in P. \ledger{38,42,53,54,55,56,57}

\begin{objection}
The visual experiment confirms that delay generally makes comparative
shaping harmful.
\end{objection}

It does not. The requested delay intervention was performed and
failed to reproduce that prediction in the visual task. Delay also
increased critic minibatch steps. The result supports task- and
optimizer-dependent finite-training effects, not a general delay
failure. \ledger{56,57}

\begin{objection}
A return-gradient audit supplies a new label-free safety mechanism.
\end{objection}

The fixed-direction estimator is a local diagnostic grounded in known
policy-gradient and gradient-alignment ideas. Sparse feedback forces
abstention, first-order tests cannot certify arbitrary finite steps,
and the measured raw gradient need not be the optimizer displacement.
No online safety algorithm or Q-style implementation result was
established. \ledger{48,49,51,56}

\begin{objection}
The contribution may only combine known shaping bias, projection
geometry, normalization failure, and contests.
\end{objection}

This remains a decisive limitation. The constructions and interventions
make the question precise, but HuRL, Difference Advantage Estimation,
normalized utility design, BARFI, gradient-similarity learning, and
decision-aware actor-critic work substantially narrow novelty.
The visual study adds evidence and negative generalization results;
it does not resolve the publication bar. \ledger{16,24,32,36,46,48,58}

\section{Outside sources and novelty limits}

The research used the shared paper pool, requesting LaTeX source
before PDFs and using cached PDF text only when source was unavailable.
No separate paper copies were downloaded into the agent folder.
Initial access failures were recorded rather than treated as completed
source comparisons. \ledger{6,14,19,32,36,48}

\begin{itemize}
\item Wiewiora (2003), \emph{Potential-Based Shaping and Q-Value
Initialization are Equivalent},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.5267}, supplies the tabular connection
between fixed shaping and shifted initialization. The helper used
cached PDF text after finding LaTeX unavailable. Fixed-initialization
equivalence does not justify an unrestricted extension to dynamic
potentials. \ledger{14}

\item Devlin and Kudenko (2012), \emph{Dynamic Potential-Based Reward
Shaping},
\url{https://www.ifaamas.org/Proceedings/aamas2012/papers/2C_3.pdf},
provides the dynamic policy and equilibrium invariance baseline.
Initial requests failed with HTTP 429; later retrieval succeeded.
\ledger{6,14}

\item Grzes (2017), \emph{Reward Shaping in Episodic Reinforcement
Learning},
\url{https://aamas.csc.liv.ac.uk/Proceedings/aamas2017/pdfs/p565.pdf},
derives initial and final boundary effects, including multi-agent
equilibrium distortion. The entry refund applies this principle to
active segments. \ledger{14}

\item Schulman et al., \emph{High-Dimensional Continuous Control Using
Generalized Advantage Estimation},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.02438}, interprets GAE through reward
shaping and additional discounting. The pooled LaTeX source,
lines 516--579, was used for comparison. The general GAE
decomposition is not a novelty claim. \ledger{14,16}

\item Li, Xie and Lu (2022), \emph{Difference Advantage Estimation for
Multi-Agent Policy Gradients},
\url{https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/li22w.html}, studies
shaping-induced credit assignment and policy bias and introduces an
estimator combining with GAE. Its abstract and indexed excerpts
establish close prior work, not a demonstrated novelty gap.
Full text remains unavailable through the recorded pool requests.
\ledger{16,19,32,36,58}

\item Cheng, Kolobov and Swaminathan (2021),
\emph{Heuristic-Guided Reinforcement Learning},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.02757}, is pooled as
\url{sources/2106.02757.tex}.
Source lines 425 onward introduce a future heuristic and shortened
discount; lines 469--487 distinguish policy invariance from learning
speed and horizon-based bias. The frozen residual has the same
algebraic short-horizon form. Neither that form nor the broad
guidance-bias tradeoff is new here. See
\url{ada/round4_critic_information.md}, lines 274--282.
\ledger{36,44}

\item Marden and Wierman's distributed welfare games analyze
marginal-contribution utilities, Shapley allocation, and normalization
failures. The available indexed manuscript includes a normalized
wonderful-life example without a pure equilibrium:
\url{https://users.cms.caltech.edu/~adamw/papers/DWG.pdf}.
The journal record is
\url{https://authors.library.caltech.edu/records/bc67q-z0253}.
Normalization failure itself is not a defensible novelty claim.
Full comparison remains incomplete. \ledger{24,32}

\item Lazear and Rosen (1981),
\url{https://doi.org/10.1086/261010}, supplies the rank-order
tournament baseline. Lazear (1989),
\url{https://doi.org/10.1086/261616}, addresses detrimental
uncooperative behavior under relative comparisons according to its
publisher abstract; full text was not obtained. Primary records for
Skaperdas and the Tullock contest tradition were also checked without
successful full-text retrieval. The softmax reparameterization as a
proportional contest is a direct calculation, not a novelty claim.
\ledger{19,32}

\item Gupta et al. (2023), \emph{Behavior Alignment via Reward Function
Optimization},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19007}, explicitly discusses
Monte Carlo potential shaping leaving expected policy-gradient
updates unchanged while possibly increasing variance. Its auxiliary
reward optimization evaluates behavior under the primary objective.
The pooled source, \url{sources/2310.19007.tex},
lines 396--416, limits novelty of the cancellation framing and
behavioral audit motivation. \ledger{48}

\item Du et al., \emph{Adapting Auxiliary Losses Using Gradient
Similarity},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.02224}, gives cosine-based gating of
auxiliary update fields. The pooled source, lines 749--775, covers
the gating rule; lines 831 and 919 discuss noisy estimates, and
lines 949 onward treat reinforcement learning. A simple alignment
audit is an application of established ideas. \ledger{48}

\item Vaswani et al., \emph{Decision-Aware Actor-Critic with Function
Approximation and Theoretical Guarantees},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.15249}, couples critic error with
permissible policy movement and improvement guarantees. The pooled
source, lines 837--895, is a close precedent for controlling actor
error through critics, though not itself an analysis of P's normalized
comparison potential. \ledger{51}

\item Maurer and Pontil, \emph{Empirical Bernstein Bounds and Sample
Variance Penalization},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.3740}, supplies the audit confidence
bound through Theorem 4, at pooled source lines 203--213.
No new concentration result is claimed. \ledger{51}

\item Thomas, Theocharous and Ghavamzadeh,
\emph{High Confidence Policy Improvement},
\url{https://proceedings.mlr.press/v37/thomas15.html}, is an existing
program for return-based policy guarantees. Only its primary abstract
page was used; no detailed theorem comparison is claimed.
See \url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md}, lines 178--188.
\ledger{51,58}
\end{itemize}

Successful early shaping-source retrievals are recorded in
\url{ada/prior-fetch-003.log}, lines 1--3.
The final network DAE request failed with HTTP 429 in
\url{ada/prior-fetch-012.log}, line 1; the HuRL retrieval is recorded
on line 2. Earlier failures remain in
\url{ada/prior-fetch-008.log},
\url{ada/prior-fetch-009.log}, and
\url{ada/prior-fetch-011.log}.
The fifth round made no further network retry. Its offline-only
check found no newly cached DAE copy; see
\url{ada/round5-offline-dae-check-001.log}, line 1.
The full-text comparison remains open. \ledger{32,36,44,58}

Public implementation sources were XuanCe and
\url{https://github.com/marlbenchmark/on-policy}, at the revisions
specified above. Earlier searches, including inspection of
\url{https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1349/}, found no
MARS-RA code link. This is a recorded search result, not proof that
public code does not exist. \ledger{8,12}

\section{What remains open and the smallest next step}

The original strategic-robustness justification remains rejected.
The accounting results are conditional applications of established
principles. The strongest surviving question is critic-dependent
comparative guidance: whether its benefit comes from information
supplied to the critic, useful transient advantage distortion, or
changed sampling variance. Q contributes the requirement to check
behavioral incentives; no new Q-style implementation theorem follows.
\ledger{20,44,58}

The previous note's proposed visual intervention has now been
performed in a small custom gridworld. It should not be presented
as unattempted. It found exact cancellation under deliberately shifted
critics, possible transient guidance under ordinary critics, and
negative evidence against generalizing the toy delay failure.
It also showed why residual RMS and averaged raw gradient alignment
do not identify a persistent mean conflict. The task-return audit
was explored with correlated components and sparse feedback, but
was not developed into an online intervention. \ledger{51,53,56,57}

P's actual critic inputs, return and termination conventions,
persistent-identity handling, score regularization, query processing,
and potential caching remain unknown. The visual study has fixed
membership, symbolic full observations, synthetic comparisons, and
three seeds per condition. It does not validate a mechanism on
MARS-Bench or a substantive public embodied benchmark. Full-text DAE
and utility-design comparisons are also incomplete.
The material remains insufficient for a publication-ready novelty
claim. \ledger{44,46,57,58}

The smallest next step toward practical relevance is a matched
intervention in one substantive task using P's implementation if
available, otherwise a validated reference stack. Hold actor
information, task objective, comparator generator, and accounting
fixed; compare input-matched unshaped, ordinary shaped, and explicitly
shifted critics. Measure the residual on common-policy rollouts and
separate predictable comparison signal from query noise, alongside
task performance and critic fitting. This would test whether the
information, transient-guidance, or variance distinction matters
outside the custom examples. It would not by itself settle novelty,
which still requires the unresolved source comparison.
Further variations of the same synthetic games are unlikely to
resolve either issue. See \url{ada/round5_visual_audit.md},
lines 219--225. \ledger{57,58}

\end{document}
